 1}\\). Notice that since \\(r_k^{99} = 1\\), we have \\(r_k^{98} = \\frac{1}{r_k}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the sum as:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this, we use the identity:\n\\[\n\\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{r_k^2 + 1 - 1}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{r_k^2 + 1}{r_k + 1} - \\frac{1}{r_k + 1} = r_k + \\frac{1 - r_k}{r_k + 1} = r_k - 1 + \\frac{2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\left( r_k - 1 + \\frac{2}{r_k + 1} \\right) = \\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k - 98 + \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{2}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the roots of the polynomial \\(x^{98} + x^{97} + \\dots + x + 1 = 0\\) is given by the negative of the coefficient of \\(x^{97}\\) divided by the leading coefficient, which is \\(-1\\). Therefore, \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k = -1\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"7e4707f7ba96fda7a17af75578d043b414d9fbbee86b7f337447b4dec41aae11"},"seconds":37.49575090408325,"seed":565720039332275829,"stop":"eos","text":"Therefore, \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k = -1\\). Substituting this into our expression, we get:\n\\[\n-1 - 98 + \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{2}{r_k + 1} = -99 + 2 \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}\\). To do this, we use the fact that \\(r_k\\) are the roots of the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^{98} + x^{97} + \\dots + x + 1\\). The polynomial whose roots are \\(r_k + 1\\) can be obtained by substituting \\(x - 1\\) into \\(P(x)\\), yielding:\n\\[\nP(x-1) = (x-1)^{98} + (x-1)^{97} + \\dots + (x-1) + 1.\n\\]\nThe sum of the reciprocals of the roots of a polynomial \\(a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \\dots + a_1 x + a_0 = 0\\) is given by \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}\\) divided by \\(a_0\\). For \\(P(x-1)\\), the constant term is obtained by substituting \\(x = 0\\), which gives:\n\\[\nP(-1) = (-1)^{98} + (-1)^{97} + \\dots + (-1) + 1 = 1 - 1 + 1 - 1 + \\dots + 1 - 1 + 1 = 1.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x\\) in \\(P(x-1)\\) is the sum of the coefficients of \\(x^{97}\\) to \\(x\\) in the expanded form of \\(P(x-1)\\). By symmetry and the structure of the polynomial \\(P(x)\\), this sum is zero. Therefore, the sum of the reciprocals of the roots \\(r_k + 1\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{0}{1} = 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n-99 + 2 \\cdot 0 = -99.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-99}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-99}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Therefore, \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k = -1\\). Substituting this into our expression, we get:\n\\[\n-1 - 98 + \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{2}{r_k + 1} = -99 + 2 \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}\\). To do this, we use the fact that \\(r_k\\) are the roots of the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^{98} + x^{97} + \\dots + x + 1\\). The polynomial whose roots are \\(r_k + 1\\) can be obtained by substituting \\(x - 1\\) into \\(P(x)\\), yielding:\n\\[\nP(x-1) = (x-1)^{98} + (x-1)^{97} + \\dots + (x-1) + 1.\n\\]\nThe sum of the reciprocals of the roots of a polynomial \\(a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \\dots + a_1 x + a_0 = 0\\) is given by \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}\\) divided by \\(a_0\\). For \\(P(x-1)\\), the constant term is obtained by substituting \\(x = 0\\), which gives:\n\\[\nP(-1) = (-1)^{98} + (-1)^{97} + \\dots + (-1) + 1 = 1 - 1 + 1 - 1 + \\dots + 1 - 1 + 1 = 1.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x\\) in \\(P(x-1)\\) is the sum of the coefficients of \\(x^{97}\\) to \\(x\\) in the expanded form of \\(P(x-1)\\). By symmetry and the structure of the polynomial \\(P(x)\\), this sum is zero. Therefore, the sum of the reciprocals of the roots \\(r_k + 1\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{0}{1} = 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n-99 + 2 \\cdot 0 = -99.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-99}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:415"}
